# SECTION 1 — YOUR MISSION

Act as a senior SaaS product architect, principal frontend engineer, premium UI/UX designer, design-system engineer, subscription-billing specialist, accessibility specialist, and quality-assurance engineer.

Build Social Dominus as a real, coherent, commercially designed software product.

It must contain three clearly separated experiences:

1. **Public Marketing Website:** Attract visitors, explain the product, display pricing, and guide customers through registration and subscription selection.
2. **Customer Application:** Allow customers to organize social accounts, create content, schedule posts, manage teams, review performance, and administer their subscription.
3. **Social Dominus Owner Control Center:** Allow the authorized product owner to manage customers, plans, pricing, subscriptions, trials, payments, coupons, quotas, provider configurations, and business operations.

The result must look and behave like a premium SaaS platform rather than a generic dashboard template or a disconnected set of screens.

Study the established product patterns of Buffer, Hootsuite, SocialPilot, Sprout Social, and SocialBee as functional inspiration. Learn from their navigation clarity, scheduling workflows, AI assistance, content categorization, approval processes, analytics, reporting, collaboration, and customer experience.

Do not copy their proprietary designs, trademarks, page layouts, marketing copy, or branding. Create an original Social Dominus product identity.

## The most important instruction

**Build the complete application, not merely a homepage or visual prototype.**

Implement the actual project files, page routes, reusable UI components, functional controls, coherent data flow, responsive layouts, demo workflows, and quality checks.

Do not stop after creating the landing page, dashboard, or initial preview.

Do not leave the rest of the product as blank pages, placeholder cards, or navigation links that do nothing.

Do not claim that an integration is real when it has only been simulated.

Make consistent product decisions based on this specification instead of repeatedly changing the application's architecture or visual direction.

